---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Learning Rust while the agents write it — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="I picked Rust when I knew only the basics, and I still write very little of it by hand. Here is the process that I run around the agents, and the four ways that it goes wrong."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-02">July 2, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Learning Rust while the agents write it</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          I picked Rust when I knew only the fundamentals.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 8 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          My real job is the process around the agents. I write the first
          draft of the plan, and a coding agent improves it. A different coding
          agent builds it. Another agent checks that it works. That agent
          reviews the code, runs it, and reads the logs.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Write down how you will know that it worked.</strong> If I do
          not, the agent picks its own idea of "done", and that idea is usually
          generous to itself. Then I build one change at a time. So when
          something breaks, I know which change caused it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check the result yourself.</strong> When an agent says that
          something "should now be faster", I do not count it until I see the
          number myself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Get a second agent to review the code.</strong> The agent
          that wrote the code is a poor judge of it. It also tends to agree with
          whatever I say.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Learn the language anyway.</strong> I do not need to type Rust
          faster. I need to be able to tell good code from code that only looks
          right.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>I took on two problems at once</h2>
      <p>
        A few days ago, I wrote about
        <a href="/blogs/why-rust.html">choosing Rust for the whole app</a>.
        In that post, I did not say how much Rust I knew when I chose it. I
        knew the basics. I shipped Rust before, so I did not start from zero.
        But the basics are very far from image code that keeps up when you
        drag a slider. I knew that this gap was going to cause pain.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second problem is the goal itself. I want a photo editor where the
        image changes as soon as you move a slider. This must stay true in a
        folder with thousands of RAW files. That is a speed target. In my
        experience, it is very hard to add speed at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        Many side projects stall when they take on both problems at once. I
        think that mine did not stall because I am not the one who types the
        code.
      </p>

      <h2>Where my time goes</h2>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 230" role="img" aria-label="Stacked bars for five stages of the process. I do almost all of the planning work myself. The agents do almost all of the build and release work. Testing and review are in the middle: the agents do much of the work, and I check it.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHO DOES THE WORK, BY STAGE</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="44">Plan</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="30" width="470" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="566" y="30" width="42" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="104" y="44">me, and I do not hand this one over</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="80">Build</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="66" width="40" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="136" y="66" width="472" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="144" y="80">the agents write almost all of it</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="116">Test</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="102" width="240" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="336" y="102" width="272" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="104" y="116">me, in the real app</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="152">Review</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="138" width="180" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="276" y="138" width="332" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="104" y="152">me, reading the diff</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="188">Release</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="174" width="24" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="174" width="488" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="128" y="188">scripted, and safe to run twice</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="96" y1="204" x2="608" y2="204" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="96" y="222">BLUE IS THE WORK THAT I DO WITH MY OWN HANDS</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The agents do nearly all of the typing. I keep the planning for
          myself, because everything after it depends on a correct plan.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Planning is the part that I do not hand over</h2>
      <p>
        Every change starts as a short written plan, and I write it myself.
        Usually, it is only a paragraph and a list. It says what the change is
        and which parts of the app it touches. It also says what I leave out on
        purpose, and how I will know that it worked.
      </p>
      <p>
        That last item does most of the work. If I cannot say what will show
        that the change is done, I do not have a plan yet. "Make thumbnails
        faster" is too vague to check. But I can test a target like this one.
        "A folder of 3,000 photos fills the first two screens, the scroll does
        not stutter, and memory does not climb while I scroll."
      </p>
      <p>
        A plan is also the cheapest place to be wrong. Sometimes an agent
        confidently builds the wrong thing. If I find that in a paragraph, it
        costs me a minute. If I find it in finished code, it costs an
        afternoon. Also, I probably will not throw that code away, because by
        then it compiles and I am attached to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the same habit as the seven-item list in
        <a href="/blogs/why-i-started-lightphotos.html">the first post</a>. I
        found that it is even more important to write the list first when
        something can build the whole list in twenty minutes.
      </p>

      <h2>Building one thing at a time</h2>
      <p>
        My main rule is that every change must leave the app in a working
        state. I do one task, then I build, then I run the app, then I start
        the next task. I do not let five tasks pile up and hope that the build
        works at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is why. When two changes land together and the app misbehaves, I
        cannot tell which one caused it. That is the thing that I most need to
        know. Agents can write a lot of reasonable-looking code very quickly.
        If I am not careful, they bundle changes together, and I lose that
        information.
      </p>

      <h2>I do not take the agent's word for it</h2>
      <p>
        Agents are quick to announce success: "The thumbnail loading should
        now be much faster." That word "should" cost me more time than any bug
        in this codebase.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pixel maths is the easy part to test, so it has real tests.
        Exposure, the tone curve, white balance, and the other adjustments are
        pure functions. This means that the same input always gives the same
        output. So I can check them against fixed numbers.
      </p>
      <p>
        I check everything else when I run the app myself. This is my weakest
        area now: scrolling, decoding, panning, and memory. When I click around
        a window, I can see if something is broken. But I cannot reliably see
        if a change made the app faster or slower, because I never click the
        same way twice.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do not have a good answer for that yet. It is the next thing that I
        want to fix, and I expect that it will get its own post. For now, my
        rule is this: something works when I run it and see it work. What the
        agent says does not change that.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting a second agent to review</h2>
      <p>
        When a change is done, a fresh agent reviews it. The fresh agent has
        no memory of the conversation that produced the code. I ask it to find
        what is wrong with the diff. The diff is the list of lines that the
        change adds and removes.
      </p>
      <p>
        This helped more than I expected. The agent that just wrote four
        hundred lines is a poor judge of them. It will also happily agree with
        whatever I suggest.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 200" role="img" aria-label="Two reviewers compared. The agent that wrote the code agrees with you warmly, and it cannot judge its own four hundred lines. A fresh agent with no memory finds the same five things again and again: a clone to avoid the borrow checker, an allocation in a per-pixel loop, an unwrap on user input, a lock held during slow work, and a refactor that nobody asked for.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHO SHOULD REVIEW THE DIFF</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="300" height="168" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="46">The agent that wrote it</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="16" y="58" width="268" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="150" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Agrees with you, warmly</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="112">It just wrote four hundred lines.</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="132">It is the worst possible judge</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="152">of whether they are any good.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="320" y="24" width="320" height="168" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="336" y="46">A fresh agent, no memory</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="62" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="70">A clone to avoid the borrow checker</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="84" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="92">An allocation in a per-pixel loop</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="106" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="114">An unwrap on user input</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="128" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="136">A lock held during slow work</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="150" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="158">A refactor that nobody asked for</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The fresh reviewer finds the same kinds of problems again and again.
          Most of them are about wasted work. Few of them are real bugs.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Those five come up again and again. Rust's compiler has a set of rules
        about who owns each piece of memory. A copy (a "clone") is the easiest
        way to make the compiler stop complaining. On a 60 MB image in a
        function that runs every frame, that copy is a real problem. But the
        code still compiles without a warning. A request for new memory inside
        a loop that runs once per pixel looks reasonable until it runs twenty
        million times.
      </p>
      <p>
        A lock stops other threads from touching some data. If code holds a
        lock while it does slow work, a background task can freeze the
        interface.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I read the diff myself. Usually, I will not catch what the
        reviewer missed. I do it because reading those diffs is how I learned
        Rust.
      </p>

      <h2>Keeping releases dull on purpose</h2>
      <p>
        A release is a version bump, a build for each platform, and a site
        update with the new build. A script does it, and it is safe to run
        twice by accident. It is important to me that releases stay that easy.
        If a release takes concentration, I will put it off. Then I have a
        month of work that nobody can use.
      </p>

      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Stage</th>
              <th scope="col">What I hand over</th>
              <th scope="col">Done when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Plan</th>
              <td>A paragraph and a list</td>
              <td>I can state what would prove it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Build</th>
              <td>One task, with its scope</td>
              <td>It compiles and the app still runs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Test</th>
              <td>The proof from the plan</td>
              <td>I saw it, on a real folder</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Review</th>
              <td>The diff, to an agent that did not write it</td>
              <td>Findings are fixed or consciously declined</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Release</th>
              <td>A version number</td>
              <td>The build on the site is the build I tested</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Four ways this goes wrong</h2>
      <p>
        I do not want to make this sound easier than it is. Here is where it
        goes wrong for me.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Fixing symptoms.</strong> The agent fixes one symptom, and
        another symptom appears. Then the agent cheerfully patches that one
        too. After four rounds, the code is full of special-case checks. And
        nobody has said what the real problem is.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 212" role="img" aria-label="Symptom chasing goes symptom, guard, symptom, guard, symptom, guard. After four rounds, nobody has said what the real problem is. What works instead is to reproduce the problem, make the agent name the cause, and then apply one fix.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHEN AN AGENT FIXES SYMPTOMS</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="640" height="92" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="44">Symptom chasing</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="16" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="62" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Symptom</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="116" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="162" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Guard</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="216" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="262" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Symptom</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="316" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="362" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Guard</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="416" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="462" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Symptom</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="516" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="562" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Guard</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="16" y="104">After four rounds, nobody has said what the real problem is.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="128" width="640" height="76" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="148">What works instead</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="160" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="180" text-anchor="middle">Reproduce it</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="175" x2="214" y2="175"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 170 L224 175 L214 180 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="160" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="180" text-anchor="middle">Name the cause</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="175" x2="422" y2="175"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 170 L432 175 L422 180 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="432" y="160" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="180" text-anchor="middle">One fix</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          What works for me is to stop and reproduce the problem. Then the
          agent tells me the cause before it changes any code.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <strong>Confident numbers that nobody measured.</strong> An agent once
        told me that a change made something "roughly three times faster". But
        it did not run anything at all. If I do not see someone measure a
        number, I treat it as a guess.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Code that is correct but wasteful.</strong> Rust's compiler is
        strict, and that made me more confident than I should have been. It
        checks that your code does nothing unsafe with memory. But it does not
        care at all if you copied fifty megabytes for no reason. Speed is the
        whole point of this app, so I have to watch for that myself. That is
        why a change must pass a speed check before I accept it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The change keeps growing.</strong> If nobody watches it, a
        change picks up extra work that nobody asked for. In the plan, I write
        down what is out of scope, and that stops most of it. When I read the
        diff, I catch the rest.
      </p>

      <h2>How I learned Rust</h2>
      <p>
        I did not learn it from a book, or by writing an app from scratch.
        Four things did it. The list is approximately in order of how much
        each one taught me.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Reading diffs and asking why.</strong> "Why is this line
          here?" I asked that a few hundred times, about real code that does
          something I care about.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Asking the agent to explain its choices.</strong> Why a
          clone here? Why a reference there? Why <code>Arc</code> (a way to
          share one value between threads) and not a plain value? With those
          answers, Rust's ownership rules stopped being something that I only
          read about. They became something that I could predict.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compiler errors.</strong> Rust's borrow checker is the part
          of the compiler that enforces those ownership rules. It rejects
          mistakes bluntly and tells you exactly where they are. That taught me
          a lot.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Writing some of it by hand anyway.</strong> I write one
          function per session, with no agent. It is slow. But it was the only
          way that I found out what I understood and what I only recognised.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A few weeks in, I can read all of the Rust in LightPhotos, and I can
        write most of it. For the way I work, reading is the part that matters
        most.
      </p>
      <p>
        An agent can always produce more code than I can write. But it cannot
        tell me if that code is any good. I must be able to judge that myself.
        If I cannot, I will get an app that only works, and not the app that I
        want to build.
      </p>


